{{Japanese name|Nakayama}}

'''Rento Nakayama''' ({{Nihongo|中山 連人|Nakayama Rento|born April 12, 1993}}) is a Sanese politician, former television actor, and social media personality who has served as the [[Deputy Prime Minister of Okaiken]] and [[Minister of Social Affairs (Okaiken)|Minister of Social Affairs]] since 20 May 2026 in the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]]. He has been the Leader of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP) since May 2024. Nakayama rose to national prominence during the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]], leading the nearly extinct socialist party from two seats to fifty in a populist electoral wave dubbed "Nakayama-mania," before expanding the OSP to one hundred seats in the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|May 2026 snap election]].

Nakayama previously served as Minister of Health and Welfare in the conservative-socialist [[Honda-Nakayama Cabinet]] from November 2025 until his dramatic public resignation in March 2026, which precipitated the collapse of the Honda government following the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]]. In the subsequent left-wing coalition government formed with Prime Minister [[Kousuke Ikazaki]], Nakayama was granted a historic "super-ministry" portfolio combining the Deputy Premiership with the newly established Ministry of Social Affairs, granting him overarching coordinating authority across public healthcare, labor protections, welfare entitlements, and passenger rail safety.

==Early life and background==

Rento Nakayama was born on 12 April 1993 in [[Okami]], the capital metropolis. Unlike many dynastic figures within the Sanese political establishment, Nakayama was raised in a working-class household in the industrial southern wards of the capital. His father was an assembly line technician at an automotive parts plant, and his mother worked as a geriatric care nurse. This personal background formed the ideological core of his political identity.

Nakayama attended Okami municipal schools and enrolled at the Okami University of Arts, graduating in 2015 with a degree in dramatic performance. Concurrently developing a passion for physical culture, he obtained credentials in athletic conditioning and sports nutrition from the Okaiken Sports Science University in 2018. Over the subsequent four years, Nakayama built a substantial media following as a fitness instructor, television commercial actor, and digital lifestyle commentator, establishing his lifestyle brand Ren-Fit.

==Political rise and the 2025 breakthrough==

Nakayama entered electoral politics in 2022, joining the battered Okaiken Socialist Party after the party had been reduced to a historical low of two parliamentary seats. Recognizing that traditional socialist party structures were moribund, Nakayama launched an audacious campaign for the party leadership in May 2024. Utilizing his extensive digital media following and charismatic rally appearances, he overwhelmed established party elders, securing the OSP chairmanship on a platform of anti-austerity, national health expansion, and working-class empowerment.

In the October 2025 general election, Nakayama led the OSP through a high-energy campaign dubbed "Nakayama-mania." Blending left-wing economic populist rhetoric with direct appeals to disaffected young workers, he attacked runaway living costs, demanded state expansion of healthcare, and criticized traditional political elites. The election produced a seismic parliamentary result: the OSP surged from two to fifty seats, while the ruling Progressive Party of Okaiken lost its absolute majority. Holding the parliamentary balance of power, Nakayama entered a tense coalition pact with conservative Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]], assuming the post of Minister of Health and Welfare.

==Health Minister and the March Maelstrom==

During his five months within the Honda-Nakayama Cabinet, Nakayama frequently clashed with conservative cabinet colleagues over fiscal austerity. When currency depreciation during the January 2026 En Crisis inflated the price of imported basic staples, Nakayama broke cabinet ranks to demand a massive emergency cost-of-living relief package. During the grueling budget committee hearings of February 2026, he conducted high-stakes backchannel talks with opposition leader [[Koichiro Sadanuma]] of the LDP, threatening to collapse the government unless Prime Minister Honda agreed to freeze scheduled healthcare premium hikes and issue targeted digital food vouchers.

The coalition reached its breaking point on 1 March 2026 following the fatal [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]], wherein a seventeen-year-old student was killed by a driver-only train operated under deregulated transit rules. Nakayama immediately declared that passenger transport safety was an inseparable component of public health and human dignity. When Prime Minister Honda refused to impose an immediate national moratorium on one-man train operations, Nakayama cleared his ministerial desk before television cameras on 11 March 2026 and withdrew the OSP from the coalition. The resulting vote of no confidence toppled the Honda administration and triggered the snap election of May 2026.

==Deputy Prime Minister and the Social Affairs Super-Ministry==

{{main|Ikazaki Cabinet|Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission}}

In the May 2026 snap election, Nakayama led the OSP to a historic triumph, doubling the party's parliamentary representation to one hundred seats. Following seventy-two hours of intense tripartite coalition negotiations with Sanese Communist Party chairman [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] and Social Democratic Party leader [[Shimane Yoshida]], Nakayama secured the formal creation of the Ministry of Social Affairs.

As Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Social Affairs, Nakayama commands a coordinating super-ministry designed to overcome bureaucratic silos. His office exercises overarching supervisory authority across the [[Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare (Okaiken)|Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare]] under Minister [[Satoshi Kato]] and the [[Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism (Okaiken)|Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism]] under Minister [[Seiran Mochizuki]].

His signature executive priority has been the nationwide implementation of the Two-Man Mandate, which outlaws single-operator trains on all high-capacity passenger corridors, and the administrative reinforcement of the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]]. Under his coordination, the government treats occupational safety, fair wages, transit safety, and universal healthcare as integrated components of the national social contract.

==Social Affairs portfolio and relations with prefectural and metropolitan governments==

A defining aspect of Nakayama's ministerial role is his extensive intergovernmental interface with the forty-seven provincial prefectures and [[Okami Metropolis]]. Because social welfare delivery, elderly healthcare, and commuter transit lines operate within local communities, Nakayama's super-ministry relies on active coordination with regional chief executives.

Nakayama established the National Social Welfare Joint Council, convening monthly with the welfare committee of the National Governors' Association. Through this channel, Nakayama directs central treasury equalisation funds to support rural prefectural Social Welfare Councils (社会福祉協議会) in aging prefectures such as [[Miyagi Prefecture]] and [[Yamagata Prefecture]]. Recognizing that rural prefectures faced severe physician and nursing shortages, Nakayama established the Regional Healthcare Stabilization Grant, funding wage bonuses for medical professionals who relocate to provincial community hospitals.

In transit safety enforcement, Nakayama works in close coordination with prefectural public safety divisions to enforce the Two-Man Mandate across regional private railway operators and third-sector rural rail lines. When regional private operators complained that staffing two crew members per train would force service abandonments, Nakayama partnered with the Ministry of Transport to provide operational safety wage subsidies directly to local train operators, ensuring that passenger safety was maintained without eliminating rural transit connections.

Relations with the [[Okami Metropolitan Government]] require continuous, high-level negotiation. Representing the first electoral district of Okami Metropolis in the House of Commoners, Nakayama maintains an influential relationship with the Governor of Okami Metropolis. Nakayama and metropolitan authorities created the Metropolitan Working Youth Support Taskforce, an initiative co-funded by the national Ministry of Social Affairs and the Okami Metropolitan Bureau of Social Welfare. The taskforce provides rent subsidies, public health clinic access, and mental health counseling for hundreds of thousands of gig-economy workers, delivery couriers, and freelance service employees concentrated across the central wards of Okami Metropolis.

